#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171796 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feb953f4-0a88-5d41-b62d-e8d7acd05801" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of an Ellipsoid Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "202b0e22-7529-5696-9e15-18ed74e11d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-8882" :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 5\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: 4\\cos\\phi\\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{36}+\\dfrac{y^2}{25}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1$.\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 6X, \\qquad y =5 Y, \\qquad z = 4Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 + Z^2 = 1\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1$.\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad Y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad Z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6X, 5Y, 4Z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 4ρ\\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 4\\cos ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)]$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$.\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}6\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171797 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e45977-275f-51ef-b8b5-f7a47c770ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Sphere Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "cd3d5e9a-ef62-5239-af97-1c6353f75f4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8883" :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle 5 \\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\text{_____}, \\: 5 \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2+y^2+z^2=25$. What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =5$.\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5 \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5 \\cdot \\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 5 \\cdot \\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 5\\cos ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)]$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$.\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5 \\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171798 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "077dde84-b034-5b84-90c2-48e37def3295" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Cone Using Cylindrical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "150bfb70-706c-53d0-8a5c-1da52f27ad3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8884" :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 6r\\sin\\theta, \\: r \\big\\rangle, \\qquad r \\geq 0$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$z^2=\\dfrac{x^2}{25}+\\dfrac{y^2}{36}, \\qquad z\\geq 0$.\n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 5X, \\qquad y = 6Y, \\qquad z = Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\nZ^2=X^2+Y^2,\\qquad Z\\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z = r$.\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad Y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad Z = Z\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r, θ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5X, 6Y, Z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5 \\cdot r\\cos θ, 6 \\cdot r\\sin θ, Z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5r\\cos θ, 6r\\sin θ, r⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: r \\in [0,\\infty)]$ and $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).]$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5r\\cos\\theta}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "077dde84-b034-5b84-90c2-48e37def3295" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Cone Using Cylindrical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e83c31e-61ba-532a-ac3a-1497b32c7003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168122} {:answer/id #uuid "df9f97a6-97f5-57f5-87ec-6a0badff5d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168123} {:answer/id #uuid "e79681a0-7526-5c40-b33d-8b7ac8cc8344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168124} {:answer/id #uuid "4a810fe9-06f1-5fb5-908d-19f89a367ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}r\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168125} {:answer/id #uuid "5e04b0d0-6f61-5468-91b6-4a2a7f10dd7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e04b0d0-6f61-5468-91b6-4a2a7f10dd7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168126} :answer-field/id #uuid "10892380-0261-5e00-95fb-9c294ae3cb70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168121}] :question/id #uuid "7902ab3b-1269-59a0-b777-47bd4ce0abe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103979" :question/problem "$f(r, θ) = ⟨_____, r\\sin θ, r⟩, r \\ge 0$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r, θ)$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z^{2} = \\frac{x^{2}}{4} + y^{2}$ for $z \\ge 0$. What is the first component of $f$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "077dde84-b034-5b84-90c2-48e37def3295" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Cone Using Cylindrical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5dab4f3-5b4e-5d5d-bf3a-c4d197526781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{4}\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168164} {:answer/id #uuid "e18ff035-aebf-577f-b395-dc2ef86780d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168165} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a12c48-5a62-5928-978e-42baf881dcc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168166} {:answer/id #uuid "0422b4d5-cced-5f80-ae5b-5ba6ecb6bb8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cos 4θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168167} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4a879e-df26-5131-8503-3f08480febf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{4}\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6a12c48-5a62-5928-978e-42baf881dcc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168166} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f40d00c-77a4-51ec-97b9-d41c575750d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168163}] :question/id #uuid "c5888ac9-e652-5536-bba8-b7c51e45c946" :question/math-academy-id "q-105722" :question/problem "$f(r, θ) = ⟨_____, 4r\\sin θ, r⟩, r \\ge 0$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r, θ)$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z^{2} = \\frac{x^{2}}{16} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16}$ for $z \\ge 0$. What is the first component of $f$?"}]}
